    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that's a good start. can you go a bit deeper on the screen reader part? specifically, how do we handle things like dynamic content updates or error messages so they don't get missed? sometimes it feels like these little errors just pop up and vanish without being announced properly.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Dynamic content updates and error messages can be challenging to ensure are properly announced by screen readers. One approach is to use ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) attributes to provide a clear indication of the content's presence and its relationship to the surrounding context.\n\nFor dynamic content updates, you can use the `aria-live` attribute to notify screen readers when new content is added or updated. This attribute can be used on elements like `div` or `span` to indicate that the content is live and should be announced by the screen reader.\n\nFor example, you can add the following attribute to a `div` element that contains dynamic content:\n```html\n<div aria-live=\"polite\" role=\"region\">\n  <!-- dynamic content here -->\n</div>\n```\nThis will notify the screen reader that the content is live and should be announced when it becomes available.\n\nRegarding error messages, you can use the `aria-describedby` attribute to provide a reference to the error message that the screen reader can use to announce the error. This attribute can be used on elements like buttons or links to provide a clear indication of the error and its location.\n\nFor example, you can add the following attribute to a button that triggers an error message:\n```html\n<button aria",
      "source": "target",
