      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the aria info. that makes sense. i'm also worried about those weird edge cases where things just... glitch out. like when a user interaction triggers something unexpected that isn't even in the standard flow, or when a bug causes the interface to behave in a way that wasn't intended. how do we account for those \"hidden\" or unpredictable issues in an accessibility audit?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those edge cases can be particularly challenging to identify and address. One approach to account for these \"hidden\" or unpredictable issues is to use a combination of automated testing and manual testing to identify potential accessibility problems.\n\nAutomated testing tools like Selenium or Cypress can be used to simulate user interactions and test the app's accessibility features. These tools can help identify issues related to dynamic content, keyboard navigation, and screen reader compatibility.\n\nHowever, automated testing alone may not be enough to catch all the edge cases. That's where manual testing comes in. You can use a combination of manual testing and user testing to identify issues that may not be caught by automated testing.\n\nFor example, you can use a user testing tool like UserTesting or TryMyUI to record a user interacting with the app and then review the video to identify any accessibility issues that may have been missed.\n\nAdditionally, you can also use a technique called \"error guessing\" to identify potential accessibility issues. This involves intentionally triggering unexpected user interactions or bugs to see if the app's accessibility features can handle the unexpected behavior.\n\nIt's also worth noting that some accessibility tools, like WAVE or Lighthouse, can provide a \"hidden\" or \"unannounced\" report that highlights potential accessibility issues that may",
